  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-10a-2003-p11.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "The two numbers equal \\(100 \\cdot \\overline{AMC} + 10\\) and \\(100 \\cdot \\overline{AMC} + 12,\\) so their sum is \\(200 \\cdot \\overline{AMC} + 22 = 123422.\\)\n\nThen \\(200 \\cdot \\overline{AMC} = 123400,\\) so \\(\\overline{AMC} = 617.\\)\n\nTherefore \\(A + M + C = 6 + 1 + 7 = 14.\\)\n\nThus, the correct answer is E.",
      "hints": [
        "Write each number as \\(100 \\cdot \\overline{AMC}\\) plus its last two digits",
        "Their sum is \\(200 \\cdot \\overline{AMC} + 22 = 123422\\)"
      ],
